course: biology
lesson: 383
title: Giant reptiles of the past: Titanoboa, Sarcosuchus, Deinosuchus and giant tortoises
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Imagine one piece of a snake's backbone bigger than your fist. Scientists found pieces like that in a coal mine in Colombia, and from them they worked out a snake about 13 metres long, as heavy as a small car: Titanoboa.
explain: Long ago there were other reptile giants too: SuperCroc in Africa with a skull as long as a grown-up is tall, Deinosuchus in North America that bit dinosaurs, sea turtles longer than a car and a giant lizard in Australia.
explain: Reptiles are cold-blooded: they get their body heat from their surroundings. Some scientists think a warmer world helped these giants grow so big, but that is still argued.
explain: Scientists often have only a few bones, so they compare them with living animals to guess the size, and they honestly say «we don't know» when the clues run out.
explain: Big animals need lots of food and have few babies, so when climates changed and rivers moved, the giants were among the first to go.
fact: Titanoboa lived in South America about 60 million years ago, soon after the dinosaurs died out, and its bones were found in a coal mine in Colombia.
fact: From its backbone pieces scientists think Titanoboa was about 13 metres long and as heavy as a small car, about twice as long as the biggest snakes today.
fact: Titanoboa lived in a warm, steamy world of rivers and may have eaten mostly big fish; whether warmth let it grow so big is still argued.
fact: A big snake has a few hundred backbone pieces, nearly each with a pair of ribs; you have 33 backbone pieces and 24 ribs.
fact: Sarcosuchus, called SuperCroc, lived in Africa about 110 million years ago, grew about 9 to 12 metres long and its fossils were found in the Sahara, which then had rivers and swamps.
fact: SuperCroc had a round bony bump at the tip of its snout, and nobody knows for sure what it was for.
fact: Deinosuchus was a giant alligator relative in North America about 80 to 75 million years ago, with thick blunt crushing teeth, and some dinosaur bones carry its bite marks.
fact: Archelon was a sea turtle about 75 million years ago, longer than a car, with four flippers and a leathery shell, like today's leatherback.
fact: Stupendemys lived in South American rivers several million years ago, had a shell over two metres long, and some shells were reported to have two horns at the front.
fact: Megalania was a giant monitor lizard in Australia until about 50,000 years ago; its size is argued, but many think about 5 to 7 metres.
fact: The saltwater crocodile, up to about 6 metres long, is the biggest living reptile.
exercise: How long do scientists think Titanoboa was? | About 13 metres | About 1 metre long | About 50 metres long | About 3 metres long
exercise: Where were Titanoboa's bones found? | In a coal mine in Colombia | Under the ice in Antarctica | In a chalk cave in southern England | In a sandy desert in western China
exercise: When did Titanoboa live? | About 60 million years ago, soon after the dinosaurs died out | At the same time as the mammoths and the cave lions of the Ice Age | Only a thousand years ago | Before there was any life on land
exercise: Why might a warm world have helped a giant snake? | Reptiles get their body heat from their surroundings | Warm air makes snakes lighter | Snakes cannot eat in cold places at all, so they starve there | Warm water has more salt
exercise: What is a snake skeleton made of mostly? | Backbone pieces and ribs | Leg bones and a big skull | Cartilage and no bones at all | One long, bendy bone from head to tail
exercise: Why does a snake have so many backbone pieces? | They let it bend, coil, climb and squeeze | They keep it warm | They help it run fast on its four short hidden legs | They store its food
exercise: Where were SuperCroc's fossils found? | In the Sahara Desert, which once had rivers | Under a city in Japan | At the bottom of the deep sea off Australia | In a frozen lake in northern Canada
exercise: What was the round bump at the end of SuperCroc's snout for? | Nobody knows for sure; it is still argued | It was a horn for digging up turtles | It was its nose that breathed fire at enemies | It was an egg it carried
exercise: What kind of teeth did Deinosuchus have? | Thick blunt teeth for crushing | Long thin needle teeth for fish | No teeth at all | Flat teeth for grinding grass and leaves
exercise: How do we know Deinosuchus attacked dinosaurs? | Some dinosaur bones carry its bite marks | Cave paintings show it | People saw it happen | Its stomach was found full of dinosaur feathers
exercise: How many flippers did Archelon have? | Four | Two | Six | Eight
exercise: What kind of shell did Archelon have? | A leathery shell over a frame of bones | A thick stone shell as heavy as a car | No shell at all | A shell of feathers
exercise: What was special about some Stupendemys shells? | They had two horns at the front | They were made of a kind of glass | They were perfectly round like balls | They glowed in the dark at night
exercise: Why is Megalania's size still argued? | Scientists have only a few bones to go on | Nobody has ever looked for it | It changed its size every year | It was invisible
exercise: Which is the biggest living reptile? | The saltwater crocodile | The Komodo dragon of Indonesia | The green anaconda of the Amazon | The leatherback turtle of the oceans
exercise: Why did giant reptiles struggle when the world changed? | They needed lots of food and had few babies | They were too clever to adapt | They could not see in daylight | They were all eaten by birds that grew bigger
test: «Titanoboa was just a big python, like 6 m, chill» What is right? | It was about twice as long, around 13 metres | Yes, it was exactly 6 metres | It was shorter than a garden hose | It was 100 metres long, as long as a football pitch
test: «snakes ain't got bones, they're like jelly» What is right? | Snakes have a few hundred backbone pieces with ribs | Snakes have no bones at all inside them | Snakes have only a skull | Snakes have 33 backbone pieces, the same as people
test: What would happen to a giant cold-blooded snake moved to a cold country? | It would be too cold to move and feed well | It would grow even bigger | It would turn warm-blooded within a few years | It would fly south
test: «a croc in the Sahara?? that's made up mate» What is right? | The Sahara once had rivers and swamps where SuperCroc lived | Crocodiles have always lived in sand, like lizards | Someone buried the bones there as a joke to fool the scientists | The bones blew there in a storm
test: Count them: Archelon and a beach turtle side by side. How many flippers in all? | Eight | Four | Six | Twelve
test: In a cartoon a giant prehistoric croc wears a crown and talks. What real clue tells us what Deinosuchus ate? | Bite marks from its teeth in dinosaur bones | Its crown shows it was king | Its words written down in very old books | Its pictures in cave paintings
test: «scientist said dunno what the snout bump was for, bad scientist lol» What is the fair view? | Saying we don't know is honest when clues are missing | A good scientist always knows | They should just guess and say it is true, so people are happy | The bump never existed
test: Count them: a lizard has 4 legs and 1 tail. How many legs and tails do 3 Megalania have together? | 12 legs and 3 tails | 4 legs and 1 tail | 9 legs and 3 tails | 12 legs and 12 tails
test: Which living reptile would you call a giant today? | A saltwater crocodile, up to about 6 metres | A garden lizard | A pet tortoise | A grass snake that is longer than your arm
test: «turtles had horns? u lying» What is right? | Some Stupendemys shells were reported to have two horns | All turtles have horns today | Only sea turtles have horns | Turtles never had anything like horns at all, ever, in history
test: Why do scientists compare a few giant bones with bones of living animals? | To work out the size and shape of the whole animal | To make the old bones grow back | To check the bones are not plastic toys made in a factory | To find the animal's name
exam: Ascertain the approximate length attributed by scientists to the serpent Titanoboa. | Thirteen metres | Three metres or less | Fifty metres or more | One metre and a half
exam: Identify the principal evidence indicating that Deinosuchus preyed upon dinosaurs. | Tooth marks corresponding to its dentition upon fossil dinosaur bones | Contemporary written accounts | Paintings upon the walls of caves | Preserved stomach contents containing feathers and fragments of bone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery of Sarcosuchus in the Sahara. | The region was formerly characterised by rivers and swamps | Crocodiles have at all times inhabited dry sand like lizards | The fossils were transported thither by wind | The animal was a desert lizard
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vulnerability of giant reptiles to environmental change? | Their great demand for food and few offspring rendered recovery difficult | Their small size made them easy prey | Their warm blood was unsuited to heat | They reproduced far too rapidly for the quantity of food available within their habitat
